650:
(a) On or after July 1, 1991, no construction may be initiated within the city for any residential building of any type which employs a:
(1) Gravity tank-type, flushometer-valve, or flushometer-tank toilet that uses more than an average of 1.6 gallons of water per flush;
(2) Showerhead that allows a flow of more than an average of 2.5 gallons of water per minute at 60 pounds per square inch of pressure;
(3) Urinal that uses more than an average of 1.0 gallons of water per flush;
(4) Lavatory faucet or lavatory replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.0 gallons of water per minute; or
(5) Kitchen faucet or kitchen replacement aerator that allows a flow of more than 2.5 gallons of water per minute.
(b) There shall be no construction of any commercial building initiated within the city for any commercial building of any type which does not meet the requirements of subsections (a)(1)—(5) of this section.
(c) The requirements of subsections (a)(1)—(5) of this section shall apply to any residential construction initiated after July 1, 1991, and to any commercial construction initiated after July 1, 1992, which involves the repair or renovation of or addition to any existing building when such repair or renovation of or addition to such existing building includes replacement of toilets or showers or both.
(Code 1976, § 9-1008; Ord. No. 91-7, 6-30-1991)

673:
(a) No person shall engage in the business of a building contractor in the city unless such person holds a valid unrevoked and unsuspended license as a qualified building contractor issued by the director of inspections.
(b) It shall be unlawful for any building contractor operating within the city or the county as a contractor to permit any one of the following acts or omissions:
(1) To contract to do any work outside the scope of operations as defined under the particular type of contractor for which he is licensed;
(2) To depart from or disregard in any material respect, the plan or specifications of a construction job without approval of the building inspector of the city;
(3) Disregard or violate in the performance of his construction job any of the building code or safety requirements, or health regulations of the city and the county;
(4) Misrepresent any material fact in his application and attendant papers in obtaining a certificate under this article;
(5) Aid or abet an unqualified person to evade provisions of this article, or allow one's certificate to be used by an unqualified person or act as an agent, partner or associate of an unqualified person with an intent to evade the provisions of this article;
(6) Fail to fulfill contractual obligations through failure to pay all creditors for material furnished or work or services performed which result in the successful termination by the courts in favor of the lienholder who files a mechanic's or materialman's lien against the property upon which work was performed in the operation of his business for which he is qualified under this article; or
(7) Abandon without legal excuse, construction projects or operations engaged in or under contract as a contractor.
(Ord. No. 97-36, § 7-1090, 6-5-1997)

701:
(a) The purpose of this article is to establish guidelines for the siting of all wireless, cellular, television and radio telecommunications towers and antennas.
(b) The goals of this article are to:
(1) Enhance the ability of the providers of telecommunications services to effectively and efficiently deliver such services to the community;
(2) Locate telecommunications towers and antennas in areas where adverse impacts on the community are minimized and promote harmonious coexistence of telecommunications towers with other land uses;
(3) Encourage the location of towers in nonresidential areas and minimize the total number of towers within residential areas;
(4) Encourage the innovative use of alternative tower structures, such as church steeples, outdoor advertising signs, electric transmission towers, etc., where technologically feasible;
(5) Encourage the design and construction of towers and antennas to minimize adverse visual impacts and promote visual quality; and
(6) Encourage the joint use or collocation of new and existing tower sites among service providers.
(Ord. No. 2000-37, art. I, 5-11-2000)

724:
The building inspector of the City of Valdosta is hereby designated and appointed to exercise the powers and duties pursuant to the terms and provisions of this article as hereinafter set forth, including but not limited to the following:
(1) The building inspector may determine that a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for current commercial industrial or business use if he finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling or structure which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety or morals of the occupants of such dwelling, building or structure, or of the occupants of neighborhood dwellings, buildings or structures; or of other residence of the city. Such conditions may include the following (without limiting the generality of the foregoing):
a. Defects therein increasing the hazard of fire, accident or other calamity;
b. Lack of adequate ventilation, light or sanitation facilities;
c. Dilapidation;
d. Disrepair;
e. Structural defects;
f. Violations of the building and housing codes of the City of Valdosta which create unsafe conditions which are dangerous or injurious to the health safety of the occupant and/or neighborhood.
(2) The building official or his designee may determine that a dwelling, building, or other structure is vacant and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon personal observation or report of law enforcement agencies and evidence of drug crimes being committed therein.
(Ord. No. 2011-38, 11-10-2011)

904:
The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Emergency management means the preparation for the carrying out of all emergency functions, other than functions for which military forces are primarily responsible; to prevent, minimize and repair injury and damage resulting from emergencies, energy emergencies, disasters or their imminent threat, of manmade or natural origin caused by enemy attack, sabotage, civil disturbance, fire, flood, earthquake, wind, storm, wave action, oil spill or other water contamination requiring emergency action to avert danger or damage, epidemic, air contamination, blight, drought, infestation, explosion, riot or other hostile action, or other causes. These functions include, without limitation:
(1) Firefighting services;
(2) Police services;
(3) Medical and health services;
(4) Rescue;
(5) Engineering;
(6) Warning services;
(7) Communications;
(8) Defense from radiological, chemical and other special weapons;
(9) Evacuation of persons from stricken areas;
(10) Emergency welfare services;
(11) Emergency transportation;
(12) Plant protection;
(13) Temporary restoration of public utility services; and
(14) Other functions related to civilian protection;
together with all other activities necessary or incidental to the preparation for and carrying out of these functions.
Cross reference— Definitions generally, § 1-2.

910:
When appointed, the emergency management director is charged with the following duties:
(1) Represent the mayor and council on matters pertaining to emergency management of a general nature. Direction and management of city department will be at the direction of the city manager;
(2) Assist city officials in organizing city department;
(3) Develop, in conjunction with city and county departments the county plan for emergency functions set forth in section 30-104, such plan to be in consonance with the state natural disaster operations plan and nuclear emergency operations plan, and shall be submitted to the governing officials of the county and the cities therein for approval, and thence to the state emergency management agency for approval;
(4) Maintain the emergency management agency and carry out the day-to-day administration of the county emergency management program, including the submission of required reports, to the state emergency management agency;
(5) Submit reports as required by governing officials in keeping with good management practices, e.g., financial, daily activity, etc.;
(6) Obtain, with the authority of governing officials, a facility to be used as the county emergency operating center; and
(7) Coordinate the activities of the county emergency operating center staff during periods of an emergency, under the supervision of the county governing officials.

912:
Upon a manmade or natural disaster, actual enemy attack upon the United States, or any other emergency which may affect the lives and property of the citizens of the city, the mayor separately, or jointly with the chairman of the county commissioners, or in their absences their legally appointed successors, may determine that an emergency or disaster exists and thereafter shall have and may exercise for such period as such emergency or disaster exists or continues the following powers:
(1) Enforce all rules, laws and regulations relating to emergency management, and to assume direct operational control over all emergency management resources;
(2) Seize or take for temporary use any private property for the protection of the public;
(3) Sell, lend, give or distribute all or any such property or supplies among the inhabitants of the county and to maintain a strict accounting of property or supplies distributed and for funds received for such property or supplies; and
(4) Perform and exercise such other functions and duties and take such emergency actions as may be necessary to promote and secure the safety, protection and well-being of the inhabitants of the county.

951:
Certification will be granted only in those areas for which the owner has the ability and expertise to manage and control the firm's operations and work.
(1) To be certified as a VSEB, an individual owner must meet the following criteria:
a. Reside in one of the counties in the Valdosta Metropolitan Statistical Area (MSA) for a minimum of one year at the time the application is submitted; or have an established business headquartered for a minimum of three years in Valdosta, and reside in one of the surrounding counties;
b. Have a personal net worth less than $750,000.00, excluding personal residence, including but not limited to, business value and assets (measured as book value), ownership in other businesses and all other assets personally owned, held in trust for the individual owner's benefit, or held by a spouse. Provided, however, that the following caps apply:
1. For capital improvement, $3,000,000.00 in annual gross receipts on average, over the immediately preceding three-year period;
2. For suppliers, $2,000,000.00 in annual gross receipts on average, over the immediately preceding three-year period;
3. For professional services, $2,000,000.00 in annual gross receipts on average, over the immediately preceding three-year period; and
4. For contractual services, $1,000,000.00 in annual gross receipts on average, over the immediately preceding three-year period.
These amounts shall be subject to annual analysis by the DCMA or his designee who, should the annual gross receipts in any category change by five percent, will recommend to the mayor and council amounts that may be more appropriate.
c. Have not been in the program for a total of more than eight years; provided, however, that participation may be increased by two, one-year periods for good cause;
d. Own and control more than 51 percent of the business entity being certified;
e. Own any license required by local, state or federal law;
f. Have expertise normally required by the industry for the field for which certification is sought;
g. Be a for-profit small business concern;
h. Not be a front, a broker or a pass-through;
i. Perform a commercially useful function typical of the field for which certification is granted;
j. Not be controlled or operate as front by non-VSEB family, former or present employers. Familial relationships where capital is provided for the business will be subject to scrutiny and possible rejection;
k. The VSEB owner(s) contributions of capital or expertise to acquire the ownership interest must be real and substantial; and
l. Be a business, including a sole proprietorship, partnership, corporation, limited liability company, or any other business or professional entity:
1. Which is at least 51 percent owned by one or more of the individuals identified in this section, the ownership of any such business that has been in existence for a year or over must have maintained such 51 percent ownership for at least one year; and
2. In the case of a publicly owned business, at least 51 percent of all classes of the stock of which is owned by one or more of such persons each of whom meets the personal net worth criteria set forth above; and
3. Be a citizen or lawfully admitted permanent resident of the United States and be compliant with the residency requirements of the VSEB program.
(2) Only a firm that is managed and controlled by a VSEB may be certified under the VSEB program. The VSEB owner(s) must actually exercise control over the firm's operations, work, management and policy. Evidence of such controls is as follows:
a. A firm must not be subject to any formal or informal restrictions that limit the customary discretion of such owner. There can be no restrictions through corporate charter provisions, bylaw provisions, contracts or any other formal or informal devices that prevent the VSEB owner, without the cooperation or vote of a nonqualifying person or entity from making any business decision of the firm.
b. The VSEB owner may delegate various areas of the management or daily operations of the firm to persons who would not qualify to be VSEBs only if such delegation is typical in the industry for such businesses. Such delegations of authority must be revocable, and the VSEB must retain the power to hire and fire any such person. The VSEB owner must have an overall understanding of, and managerial and technical competence, experience and expertise, directly related to the firm's operations and work.
c. The VSEB owner cannot engage in outside employment or other business interests that conflicts with the management of the firm or prevents the owner from devoting sufficient time and attention to the affairs of the firm to manage and control its activities unless such activities would be appropriate with commensurate businesses, in order to avoid sham or fraudulent certifications.
(3) Only an independent firm may be certified as a VSEB. An independent firm is one whose viability does not depend on its relationship with another firm. Recognition of an applicant as a separate entity for tax or corporate purposes is not necessarily sufficient to demonstrate that a firm is independent. In determining whether an applicant is an independent business, the VSEB program coordinator will:
a. Scrutinize relationships with non-VSEBs in such areas as personnel, facilities, equipment, financial and/or bonding support, and other resources.
b. Consider whether present or recent family, or employer/employee relationships between the applicant compromises the applicant's independence.
c. Examine the applicant's relationships with non-VSEB prime contractors to determine whether a pattern of exclusive or primary dealings with a prime contractor compromises the applicant's independence.
d. Consider the consistency of relationships between the applicant and non-VSEBs with normal industry practices.
e. An owner shall be certified only for specific types of work for which the owner(s) has the ability and expertise to manage and control the firm's operations and work.
(Ord. No. 2009-40, 9-24-2009)

